<figure>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 760 360" role="img" aria-labelledby="ca-t ca-d" class="fig-dark" style="background:#131736">
    <title id="ca-t">How a great-circle distance is measured</title>
    <desc id="ca-d">A cross-section of the Earth. Lines run from the center to points A and B on the surface. The angle between them, multiplied by the Earth's radius, gives the length of the arc from A to B.</desc>
    <circle cx="250" cy="180" r="150" fill="#1a2050" stroke="#5560a8" stroke-width="1.5" />
    <path d="M250 180 L143.9 73.9 M250 180 L397.7 153.9" stroke="#aab0dc" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6" />
    <path d="M143.9 73.9 A150 150 0 0 1 397.7 153.9" fill="none" stroke="#ff5fb4" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round" />
    <path d="M221.7 151.7 A40 40 0 0 1 289.4 173.1" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" />
    <circle cx="250" cy="180" r="4.5" fill="#fff" />
    <g font-family="Archivo Variable, Archivo, system-ui, sans-serif">
      <circle cx="143.9" cy="73.9" r="14" fill="#fff" stroke="#c4157a" stroke-width="4" /><text x="143.9" y="79" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="800" fill="#161b3d">A</text>
      <circle cx="397.7" cy="153.9" r="14" fill="#fff" stroke="#c4157a" stroke-width="4" /><text x="397.7" y="159" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="800" fill="#161b3d">B</text>
      <text x="262" y="140" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">c</text>
      <text x="170" y="150" font-size="15" fill="#aab0dc">R</text>
      <text x="236" y="206" font-size="13" fill="#aab0dc">Earth's center</text>
      <text x="470" y="120" font-size="15" fill="#aab0dc">c is the angle at the center,</text>
      <text x="470" y="143" font-size="15" fill="#aab0dc">measured in radians.</text>
      <text x="470" y="186" font-size="15" fill="#aab0dc">R is the Earth's radius,</text>
      <text x="470" y="209" font-size="15" fill="#aab0dc">6,371 km on average.</text>
      <text x="470" y="262" font-size="26" font-weight="800" fill="#fff">distance = R × c</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>Every straight line distance on a sphere comes down to one angle. The haversine formula is a reliable way to get that angle from two pairs of coordinates.</figcaption>
</figure>
